Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.540
- [Instructor] So we're told that p of x

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.010
is equal to this expression here,

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:06.300
and it says plot all the
zeros or x-intercepts,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.750
of the polynomial in
the interactive graph,

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.760
and the reason why it
says interactive graph

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.010
is this is a screenshot from this type

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:14.230
of exercise on Khan Academy,

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.780
and on Khan Academy, you'd be
able to click on points here

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:17.760
and it'd put little dots

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.210
and you can either delete them
and put 'em someplace else,

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:21.370
so it'd be an interactive graph.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:22.480
But this is just a screenshot,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.440
so I'm just going to draw on top of this.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.240
But the main goal is, what are
the zeros of this polynomial

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.300
and then you just to
plot it on this graph,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.300
so pause this video and have a go at it.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.760
All right, now to figure out
the zeros of a polynomial,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.270
you would essentially have
to figure out the x values

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.620
that would make the
polynomial equal to zero.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.860
Or another way to think
about it is the x values

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.520
that would make this equation true.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.090
X to the third plus x squared

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:52.470
minus nine x minus nine is equal to zero.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:53.570
Now, the best way to do that

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.830
is to try to factor this expression.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.760
Now, this is a third degree polynomial,

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.920
which isn't always so easy to factor,

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.000
so let's see how we might approach it.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.430
The first thing I look for is
are there any common factors

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.360
to all of these terms, and it
doesn't look like there is.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.480
The next thing I could look for,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.930
I could think about whether factoring

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:16.180
by grouping could work here,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:17.700
and when I think about
factoring by grouping,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.940
I would look at the first
two terms and I would

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.937
look at the last two terms,

00:01:22.937 --> 00:01:25.130
and I would say, is there
anything I could factor out

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:28.500
of these first two terms that would,

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:30.090
or what's the most that I could factor out

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.170
of these first two terms,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.410
and then what's the most
that I could factor out

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:33.480
of these last two terms,

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.410
and then it would leave

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.000
something similar once
I've done that factoring.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.910
Now, what I mean is, for these first two,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.850
we have a common factor of x squared,

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.530
so let's factor out an x squared

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:48.290
and these first two terms become
x squared times x plus one,

00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:51.180
and then for these second two terms,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.510
I can factor out a negative nine,

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:58.510
so I could rewrite it as
negative nine times x plus one.

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.470
Now, that all worked out quite nicely,

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.660
because now we see, if we view,

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.380
if we view this as our now our first term

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.000
and this as our second term,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:14.000
we can see that x plus one
is a factor of both of them.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.950
And so we can factor that out.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.900
We can factor out the x plus one,

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.040
and I'll do that in this light blue color,

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.730
actually let me do it with
slightly darker blue color.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.810
And so if you factor out the x plus one,

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:31.810
you're left with x plus
one times x squared,

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.460
x squared, minus nine.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.100
Minus nine.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.710
And that is going to be equal to zero.

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.530
Now, we are not done factoring yet,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.770
because now we have a
difference of squares.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.510
X squared minus nine, this
is going to be equal to,

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.010
and let me just write it all out,

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.320
so I have this x plus one here,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.320
so I have x plus one, and
then the x squared minus nine,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:02.610
I can write as x plus
three times x minus three.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.930
If any of what I'm doing
feels unfamiliar to you,

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.490
if that first factoring feels unfamiliar,

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.880
I encourage you to review
factoring by grouping,

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.010
and if what I just did looks unfamiliar,

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.230
I encourage you to look at
factoring differences of squares.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:19.840
But anyway, all of that
would be equal to zero.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.930
Now, if I have the product of
several things equaling zero,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.560
any, if any one of those
things is equal to zero,

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.840
that would make the whole
expression equal to zero.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.320
So we have a situation where one

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:32.380
solution would be the solution

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.350
that makes x plus one equal to zero,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.290
and once again, I'm gonna
do that in darker color,

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:39.700
x plus one equal zero, and

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.380
that of course is x is
equal to negative one.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.470
Another solution is what would make x

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.220
plus three equal to zero?

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.550
And that of course is x is
equal to negative three,

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.160
subtract three from both sides,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.770
and then another solution is

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:58.680
going to be whatever x value
makes x minus three equal

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.250
to zero, add three to both sides,

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.600
you get x is equal to three.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:03.630
So there you have it.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:05.450
We have our three zeros.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:06.980
Our polynomial evaluated

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.330
at any of these x values
will be equal to zero.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:13.740
So we can plot it here on
this interactive graph,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.150
I'm just gonna draw on it.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.840
So we have x equals negative one,

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.860
which is right over there,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.980
x equals negative three,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.700
which is right over there,

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.780
and x equals three, which
is right over there.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:28.810
And the reason why you might
want to do this type of thing,

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:30.660
this exercise just asks us to do this,

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.270
and we're done, but the reason why this

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.140
is useful is this can help
inform what the graph looks like.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.090
This tells us where our
graph intersects the x-axis.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:41.503
So our graph might do something like this,

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:47.600
or it might do something like this,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:48.800
and we would have to look

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.470
at other information to think
about what that might be.

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.793
But I'll leave you there.

